experiment: ctxself
  SHORT RUN — 200000 ticks against a minimum of 3400000. derived: areax's session length, because it runs areax's arms plus two.
  DNA v52: the last oracle removed. areax's index is written by the HOST
  from the word label; this reads it from the creature's own larynx and
  scores it against the oracle arm measured IN THE SAME RUN. Reports the
  index's own accuracy p, and refuses if the oracle arm did not reproduce
  areax or if the derived index never names a second slice
  Everything below is printed for debugging and is NOT a
  measurement; this run cannot pass whatever it reads.

  instrument        ctxself   trial seed 00000000013579d6   71 trials per arm
                    another probe is another trial sequence: its numbers
                    are not comparable with these (~0.05 at 300 trials)
  question          `areax` proved a context-indexed bias works, keyed on
                    an index the HOST wrote from the word label. Can the
                    creature derive that index for ITSELF? v52 read it
                    off the larynx and was refused; source 4 reads the
                    ear's own rate EMA, which is what replicated.
  the bar           an index right with probability p writes the other
                    table 1-p of the time, so the conditional part scales
                    as (2p - 1). `ctxsrc` bounds p at 0.740 for ANY readout
                    of the larynx in this window, which is 48% of what the
                    oracle index bought. A FIXED partition can only do
                    worse, so that is a ceiling and not a prediction.
  arm               all four get the same host drive on the context
                    module, so `self` and `oracle` differ in ONE field.

